# Which built image every NAME resolves to. One file, so a version bump is one edit.
#
# The problem this exists to stop: nothing pointed at an image by role, so every consumer spelled
# a version. 169 campaign .env files still say optarena-amd-mi300-v4 and canon_column.sh says v5,
# which is not a decision anyone made -- it is where each file happened to stop being edited. A
# results table then cites an EDF name that means a different image depending on when it was run.
#
# So: consumers name LATEST, this file says what latest is, and promotion is a one-line change
# here followed by install_edfs.sh. Nothing names a version -- see below, there are none left to
# name, and a run that spells one silently opts out of every fix promoted after it was written.
#
# The DIGEST remains the identity of a build. LATEST is a convenience for launching, never the
# thing a results table quotes; build.sh writes <sqsh>.digest for that.

# ONE VERSION PER ROLE. Every name here is unversioned and every rebuild replaces it, so there is
# never a v2 and a v8 to reconcile: the .sqsh basename says which image it is and the .digest says
# which build. A build writes <role>-candidate.sqsh and build.sbatch refuses to write over an image
# an EDF mounts, so promotion is verify-then-rename and a running arm never reads a half-written
# squashfs.
#
# What this gives up, deliberately: there is no version to pin a run to. A results table cites the
# DIGEST, which was always the identity anyway -- a moving tag over two different images is what
# made citing one meaningless.

# Judge + agent. ONE image serves both the CPU arms and the GPU arms -- there is no separate CPU
# build, and the GPU entries below are inference engines, not a second judge image.
JUDGE_AGENT_AMD_SQSH="optarena-ce-amd-mi300.sqsh"
JUDGE_AGENT_AMD_EDF_LATEST="optarena-amd-mi300-latest"
JUDGE_AGENT_AMD_TEMPLATE="judge-agent-amd/edf.toml.example"

# The JUDGE role, split out 2026-09-09. It is the agent image plus hpcagent_bench, built as a
# second --target of the SAME Dockerfile, so the two cannot drift and building both costs one
# agent build plus a pip layer.
#
# Why it is a separate image at all: installing hpcagent_bench ships hpcagent_bench/benchmarks --
# 3850 reference files the agents are graded against -- so a single fused image cannot both give
# the judge the library and keep it from the agents. The judge stage carries the
# trusted-judge-image firewall marker; held-out hidden_tests stay out of BOTH images and are read
# from the host mount.
JUDGE_AMD_SQSH="optarena-ce-judge-amd-mi300.sqsh"
JUDGE_AMD_EDF_LATEST="optarena-judge-amd-mi300-latest"
JUDGE_AMD_TEMPLATE="judge-agent-amd/edf.judge.toml.example"

# Inference, the two engines campaigns mount: sglang serves qwen38 and kimi, vllm serves oss120b
# because the mxfp4 path gpt-oss needs is what its 0.23.0 has.
#
# Both now name an image this repo builds from a single Dockerfile. Until 2026-09-08 they named
# upstream pulls produced by a multi-phase chain, and what sglang/Dockerfile and vllm/Dockerfile
# build had never been promoted into service. That is why the sglang arms still carried
# PYTHONPATH=${SCRATCH}/pyprefix for cupy and flydsl: the served image did not have them. The
# pulls also carried a squashfs sha256 and no .digest, so publishing one published something the
# repo could not rebuild. They were deleted on 2026-09-08 along with their EDFs.
INFERENCE_SGLANG_SQSH="${INFERENCE_SGLANG_SQSH:-optarena-sglang.sqsh}"
INFERENCE_SGLANG_EDF_LATEST="sglang-latest"
INFERENCE_SGLANG_TEMPLATE="sglang/edf.toml.example"
INFERENCE_VLLM_SQSH="${INFERENCE_VLLM_SQSH:-optarena-vllm.sqsh}"
INFERENCE_VLLM_EDF_LATEST="vllm-latest"
INFERENCE_VLLM_TEMPLATE="vllm/edf.toml.example"

# vLLM 0.27.1 was RETIRED on 2026-09-08 and lives on the parked/vllm-0271 branch. Measured on one
# pinned node, same probe and parsers, it served oss120b at 2405 tok/s against 0.23.0's 3013 --
# 25% slower, and the gap is entirely in decode (steady-state 2540 vs 3187 tok/s; prefill matched
# to 0.3%). Same dtype, quantization, MoE and attention backends, same torch and triton, so it was
# vLLM's own decode path. Restore the branch rather than re-deriving this.

# PROMOTION: build to <role>-candidate.sqsh with build_and_verify.sbatch, then
#
#     ./promote_image.sh --all            (DRY_RUN=1 to see what would move)
#
# which renames the candidate over the live name above, carries the .digest, .sha256 and .oci.tar
# sidecars with it, and re-runs install_edfs.sh with ALLOW_REPOINT=1. It REFUSES a candidate with
# no .verified marker, which only a clean build_and_verify verdict writes.
#
# The rename is safe while arms are running -- a mounted squashfs is held by its inode, so a job
# that already started keeps reading the bytes it opened and only new jobs see the new image.
#
# The sidecars must move WITH the image. They are the only record of WHICH build a name currently
# holds, and with one version per role there is nothing else to tell two apart.

# Registry: ONE repository for every image -- https://hub.docker.com/r/spcleth/hpcagent-bench
#
# The tag is the .sqsh basename minus the optarena- prefix, so it names the ROLE --
# ce-amd-mi300, ce-judge-amd-mi300, sglang, vllm -- and a published tag cannot drift from the
# file it was built from.
# These tags MOVE, one per role, exactly like the .sqsh they mirror. push_image.sh publishes a
# sha-<digest> tag alongside each: cite that, never the moving one.
REGISTRY_REPO="${REGISTRY_REPO:-docker.io/spcleth/hpcagent-bench}"
sqsh_tag() { local b="${1##*/}"; b="${b%.sqsh}"; printf '%s' "${b#optarena-}"; }
JUDGE_AGENT_AMD_REPO="${REGISTRY_REPO}"
JUDGE_AMD_REPO="${REGISTRY_REPO}"
INFERENCE_SGLANG_REPO="${REGISTRY_REPO}"
INFERENCE_VLLM_REPO="${REGISTRY_REPO}"
JUDGE_AGENT_AMD_TAG="$(sqsh_tag "${JUDGE_AGENT_AMD_SQSH}")"
JUDGE_AMD_TAG="$(sqsh_tag "${JUDGE_AMD_SQSH}")"
INFERENCE_SGLANG_TAG="$(sqsh_tag "${INFERENCE_SGLANG_SQSH}")"
INFERENCE_VLLM_TAG="$(sqsh_tag "${INFERENCE_VLLM_SQSH}")"

# GETTING THE IMAGES: pull is the DEFAULT, build is the option.
#
# A rebuild is one node for hours -- the judge+agent image bootstraps gcc 16 and then llvm 22
# before it reaches PETSc and MAGMA. A pull is bandwidth, and it gets the SAME BYTES rather than a
# new build that happens to share a Dockerfile. For anyone reproducing this work, that difference
# is the whole point: the digest they cite is the digest we published.
#
#   sbatch containers/cluster/ce-images/pull_images.sbatch          # all four roles
#   ./pull_image.sh judge-agent-amd sha-<digest>                    # one, pinned
#
# Build when you are CHANGING an image, or when a role has not been published yet:
#
#   sbatch --export=ALL,IMAGE_DIR=$PWD/judge-agent-amd build_and_verify.sbatch
